The user wants to add a chaining/composition pattern to `Data.Preprocessor<T>` so multiple preprocessors can be composed sequentially. This is the classic pipeline/chain-of-responsibility pattern on a functional interface.

The idiomatic Java approach is to add a default method `andThen(Preprocessor<T> next)` that returns a new `Preprocessor<T>` which applies `this` first, then `next`. This is exactly what `java.util.function.Function.andThen` does.

Design:
```java
interface Preprocessor<T> {
    T process(T input);

    default Preprocessor<T> andThen(Preprocessor<T> next) {
        return input -> next.process(this.process(input));
    }
}
```

This allows chaining multiple preprocessors together in sequence. To demonstrate this pattern, I'll add another concrete preprocessor like `MinLengthPreprocessor` that validates the array has at least 2 elements, which is necessary for the two-pointer algorithm to work. The `TwoPointers` constructor already accepts a `Data.Preprocessor<int[]>` parameter, so this fits naturally into the existing design.

In the main method, I can compose the preprocessors using the fluent interface:

```java
Data.Preprocessor<int[]> preprocessor = new Data.SortedArrayPreprocessor()
    .andThen(new Data.MinLengthPreprocessor(2));
```

Since `andThen` is a default method on the interface itself, this creates an elegant, self-contained solution without needing a separate wrapper class for composition.

